  "_note": "DENSITY over time. Nothing varies but the hour -- ideally run across a busy->quiet transition -- so deliverability variation is attributable to ambient relay density alone.\n\nREBUILT 2026-08-08. The original was 120 repeats of the anchor alone (8 broadcasts, +9 dBm). Run 2 measured that exact condition at 100.0%, so every cell would have delivered fully however quiet the night became and the run would have produced a flat line. An anchor is chosen to be STABLE, which is right for a cross-run reference and exactly wrong for a probe.\n\nThree conditions x 40 reps, dwell held at 8000 ms throughout so only broadcasts and power differ:\n  a###_ref  adv 1000, +9 dBm,  8 broadcasts -- run 2: 100.0%. Stability reference and the chain back to runs 1/1b/2, which all read 99.4%.\n  d###_p04  adv 2000, -24 dBm, 4 broadcasts -- run 2: 83.3%. Fragile, headroom both ways.\n  d###_p02  adv 4000, -24 dBm, 2 broadcasts -- run 2: 66.1%. Steepest part of the curve, most sensitive to ambient change; two probes guard against picking the wrong fragility.\n\nCondition order rotates one slot per block so none holds a fixed position in the hour. Read the probes against the anchor: if the anchor holds near 100% while the probes fall, the change is ambient density and not the instrument or the board.\n\nThis is the main external-validity test of the series -- whether '>=8 broadcasts per key' is a general rule or a rule about this location at this hour. ANTENNA FITTED.",
